

LAST(1)              UNIX Programmer's Manual                LAST(1)



NAME
     last - indicate last logins of users and terminals

SYNOPSIS
     last [ -f file ] [ -number ] [ name ... ] [ tty ... ]

DESCRIPTION
     Last reads the login-accounting log /etc/wtmp and lists, most
     recent first, every session it holds: for each it prints the
     user, the terminal, the remote host if any, the login time,
     and the logout time with the length of the session in
     parentheses.  A session still open shows "still logged in"
     in place of the logout, and one cut short by a reboot or a
     crash shows "down" or "crash".

     A line whose terminal is `~' is a system boot, written by
     init(8); last prints it as "reboot".  Every session open at
     that instant is taken to have ended there.

     With no name or terminal argument last reports every session.
     Given names or terminals, it restricts the report to those:
     a name matches the user, and a terminal matches the line.  A
     bare terminal such as `p0' is taken as `ttyp0'; an argument
     that names a user in the password file is matched as a name.

     The options are:

     -f file      Read the accounting records from file rather than
                  from /etc/wtmp.

     -number      Stop after number lines have been printed.

     An interrupt (the INT signal, typically Ctrl-C) makes last
     print how far back into the log it has reached and stop; a
     quit is armed the same way but is not delivered on FGX.

     When the log is exhausted last prints the time of its oldest
     record as "wtmp begins".

FILES
     /etc/wtmp     the login and logout accounting log

SEE ALSO
     ac(8), who(1), w(1), login(1), init(8), utmp(5)

NOTES
     Accounting is kept only while /etc/wtmp exists, which is how it
     is turned on and off; FGX ships the file empty.  The log grows
     without bound -- there is no cron to roll it over -- so a site
     that wants it short truncates or recreates the file itself.

     A login name longer than eight characters is truncated in the
     accounting record and therefore here; see utmp(5).

